1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,812
A vial of solution doesn't have one clock.

2
00:00:02,812 --> 00:00:03,906
It has two.

3
00:00:03,906 --> 00:00:05,742
Clock one: chemistry.

4
00:00:05,742 --> 00:00:08,763
In solution, the peptide slowly changes.

5
00:00:08,763 --> 00:00:13,164
Reactions like hydrolysis and oxidation never fully stop.

6
00:00:13,164 --> 00:00:15,000
Cold slows this clock down.

7
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,523
But it never resets.

8
00:00:16,523 --> 00:00:18,645
Clock two: microbiology.

9
00:00:18,645 --> 00:00:22,734
Every time a vial is opened, contamination becomes possible.

10
00:00:22,734 --> 00:00:25,768
Preservatives help suppress bacterial growth.

11
00:00:25,768 --> 00:00:27,382
They don't stop the chemistry.

12
00:00:27,382 --> 00:00:28,515
Two clocks.

13
00:00:28,515 --> 00:00:29,843
Two different risks.

14
00:00:29,843 --> 00:00:31,653
One changes the molecule.

15
00:00:31,653 --> 00:00:33,567
One contaminates the vial.

16
00:00:33,567 --> 00:00:34,856
And here's the trap.

17
00:00:34,856 --> 00:00:36,705
The famous twenty-eight days?

18
00:00:36,705 --> 00:00:39,804
That number belongs to the microbiology clock.

19
00:00:39,804 --> 00:00:42,382
It says nothing about the peptide's chemistry.

20
00:00:42,382 --> 00:00:43,789
Both clocks are running.

21
00:00:43,789 --> 00:00:45,026
Follow us for more.

